WEBVTT
00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:04.890
<v Ira Glass>From the WBEZ Chicago, it's Your Radio Playhouse.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是“您的广播剧场”。

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:16.760
<v Calvin Bridges>Hi. My name is Reverend Calvin Bridges. And I'm the minister of music of at the Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church. And the music that you will hear tonight is being provided by the Faith Tabernacle Voices and the Faith Tabernacle Youth Voices.
<v Calvin Bridges>大家好。我叫卡尔文·布里奇斯牧师。我是信仰会幕浸信会教堂的音乐牧师。今晚你们将听到的音乐由信仰会幕之声和信仰会幕青年之声共同献上。

00:00:16.760 --> 00:01:54.840
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[CHOIR SINGING]
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[唱诗班合唱]

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:16.120
<v Ira Glass>Your Radio Playhouse . Well, it's a special treat tonight to bring you music, over the course of the hour, from the Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church down at 82nd and Stony on Chicago's South Side. Our thinking was that by this point in the Christmas season, people are pretty much tired of the regular Christmas records. So we thought that we would bring different music.
<v Ira Glass>您的广播剧场。今晚我们为您带来一小时的特别节目，音乐来自位于芝加哥南区第82街和斯托尼大道的信仰会幕浸信会教堂。我们的想法是，到了圣诞节这个阶段，人们对常规的圣诞唱片已经感到厌倦了。所以我们想带来一些不一样的音乐。

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:53.610
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Also this evening, we have amazing new stories by Reginald Gibbons, and Beau O'Reilly, and others. We have the debut of a new radio theater company. How often do you really get to say those words in 1990? A new radio theater company created by writer David Sedaris, his sister Amy, a bunch of other people. They call themselves the Pine Tree Gang.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天晚上，我们还有雷金纳德·吉本斯、博·奥莱利等人创作的精彩新故事。我们还要介绍一个新广播剧团的首演。在1990年，你们能听到多少次这样的开场白呢？这是一个由作家大卫·塞达里斯、他的妹妹艾米以及其他几个人共同创立的广播剧团。他们自称“松树帮”。

00:02:53.610 --> 00:04:17.990
<v Ira Glass>You know, the thing about Christmas is, that a Christmas, people are like themselves but more so. You know the saying that you can tell a lot about a person in a crisis? You can tell a lot about a person at Christmas also. That is not because Christmas is a crisis. As one of the producers on this program, Nancy Updike, has suggested this week, it's because Christmas is the one day in the American year when, more than any other, we are, all of us, handed exactly the same stage props with which to manufacture a day. The same expectations, the same presents, the same tree, the same idea of the meal, all that is handed to all of us. And everybody is trying to fashion a day out of exactly the same materials. And when people do that, you could see exactly who chooses, what they choose, and why. At Christmas, the kind hearted have an event on which they can exercise their kind heartedness. The impatient have more reason to be impatient. The manipulative and controlling have a set of meals and gifts with which to manipulate others. Everybody is themselves but more so.
<v Ira Glass>你知道，关于圣诞节的一点是，在圣诞节，人们还是原来的自己，但表现得更为极致。你知道那句老话吗，一个人在危机时刻的表现能说明很多问题？其实在圣诞节也是如此。这并不是因为圣诞节是一场危机。正如本节目的一位制作人南希·厄普代克本周所言，这是因为圣诞节是美国一年中，所有人比在任何时候都被赋予完全相同的“舞台道具”来过节的一天。同样的期望，同样的礼物，同样的圣诞树，同样的聚餐概念，这一切都被分发给所有人。每个人都在试图用完全相同的材料来过好这一天。当人们这样做时，你就能清楚地看到谁选择了什么，以及为什么要这样选择。在圣诞节，心地善良的人有一个可以施展爱心的场合。急躁的人有更多理由变得急躁。爱操纵和控制他人的人有一套聚餐和礼物来支配他人。每个人都是他们自己，但表现得更为极致。

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:41.950
<v Ira Glass>Which brings us to our radio play. Writer David Sedaris had an odd experience in his life. Namely, one of the first, big, public things that he did in his life is, he went on to National Public Radio and told the story of how he had worked as an elf at Santa Land at Macy's Department Store for two Christmases.
<v Ira Glass>这就引出了我们的广播剧。作家大卫·塞达里斯生活中曾有过一次奇特的经历。那就是，他人生中第一次重大的公开活动，就是登上国家公共广播电台，讲述他在梅西百货的圣诞乐园当了两个圣诞节精灵的故事。

00:04:41.950 --> 00:05:38.880
<v Ira Glass>And pretty much for the course of the two years which followed, every Christmas he would get lots of calls from magazines, and newspapers, and TV, and radio. People who wanted to hear yet another Christmas story, wanted him to unfold another Christmas story, bring one out. Here's the Christmas guy. You know, here's the Christmas guy. And these requests, they inundated him and confounded him. No one wants to be considered, for the rest of their life, as an elf. And so I think that was part of the inspiration for his radio play this evening. A premier radio play of our new radio group here at Your Radio Playhouse . The Pinetree Gang.
<v Ira Glass>在那之后的两年里，每个圣诞节他都会接到大量杂志、报纸、电视和广播电台的电话。人们想听另一个圣诞故事，想让他再讲一个圣诞故事出来。这儿有“圣诞先生”。你知道，这儿有位“圣诞先生”。这些请求让他感到不知所措，也让他倍感困扰。没有人愿意余生都被视为一个“精灵”。所以我认为这也是他今晚这部广播剧的灵感来源之一。这是我们“您的广播剧场”新广播剧团的首部广播剧——松树帮。

00:05:38.880 --> 00:06:31.190
<v Playing Gene Larkin>Last Christmas I decided to splurge and treat myself to a really extravagant present, something I'd always wanted, myself. Not who I wish I was, or what others wanted me to be. No. Just plain old me. No fancy extras, no cash return, no exchange, just myself, Gene Larkin, wrapped up in a bright, brown ribbon of self acceptance. Take it or leave it. Leaving it, saying, no to myself was a pattern as old as the checkerboard plaid I used to cover my windows. I decided to break my old pattern and set fire to my curtains, allowing both myself and others to start dealing with the real me. The me who asks, who says you can't drive to work in a go kart when you feel like it? The me who says, let's start cooking with grapes for a change.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>去年圣诞节，我决定挥霍一下，给自己买了一份真正奢华的礼物，一份我一直想要的东西。不是别人希望我成为的样子，也不是别人想要我成为的样子。不。就是单纯的我。没有花哨的额外赠品，不能退款，不能退换，只有我自己，吉恩·拉金，包裹在一条明亮的棕色自尊自爱的丝带里。你要也好，不要也罢。以前，我总是对自己说“不”，这就像我用来遮住窗户的格子窗帘一样，是一个陈旧的模式。我决定打破这个旧模式，把窗帘烧掉，让自己和别人都开始面对真实的我。那个会问，谁说你想开卡丁车去上班就不能开的？那个会说，我们要不要换换口味，改用葡萄做菜的我。

00:06:31.190 --> 00:07:10.040
<v Playing Gene Larkin>I went through my life, removing all the barricades that said, no, you can't do that. Because me, I can do anything I want. One of the things I can do is type. And another is to think about myself and my needs. I just never had the time or the opportunity to really knuckle down and practice my craft. After they had fired me for parking my go kart in the boss's handicapped space, I found myself with a lot of extra time on my hands. And the opportunity, it came by way of the Center for Annex Learning, a former indoor skating facility the city fathers have turned into what they call a school without walls.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>我走过我的人生，拆除了所有写着“不，你不能那样做”的栅栏。因为我，我可以做任何我想做的事。我可以做的事情之一就是打字。另一件事是思考我自己和我的需求。我只是从来没有时间和机会真正静下心来练习我的技艺。在我因为把卡丁车停在老板的残疾人车位而被解雇后，我发现自己空出了很多时间。而机会通过“安内克斯学习中心”到来，这是一个被市政府改造成所谓的“无墙学校”的室内滑冰场。

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:35.530
<v Playing Gene Larkin>The classrooms are separated by curtains, but that's just for the sake of privacy. The real walls are in our minds. And the center is devoted to helping us tear them down brick by brick. I can't stand walls, not anymore. You should see what I've done with my house. I'm still working on it. But in the meantime, I dropped by the Center and signed up for a course called, "Writing for the Holidays." Because, as the teacher said,
<v Playing Gene Larkin>教室之间用窗帘隔开，但这只是为了隐私。真正的墙在我们的脑海里。而该中心致力于帮助我们一块砖一块砖地拆除这些墙。我受不了墙，再也受不了了。你应该看看我对我的房子做了什么。我还在努力改造它。但在此期间，我去了中心，报了一门课叫“假期写作”。因为，正如老师所说，

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:47.130
<v Playing Teacher>Chances are, if you've got a story to tell, you've got a story to sell. How many of you would like that [JINGLING BELLS] jingling sound to come from your pockets? Would you like that?
<v Playing Teacher>只要你有故事要讲，你就很可能有一个故事能卖出去。你们中有多少人希望口袋里发出那种[摇铃声]清脆的响声？想吗？

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:48.580
<v Playing Irish Student>I sure would love it.
<v Playing Irish Student>我当然很想。

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:50.040
<v Playing Woman 1>Love it.
<v Playing Woman 1>想。

00:07:50.040 --> 00:08:19.510
<v Playing Teacher>If it sounds too good to be true, then think again. Picture all the magazines and newspapers in this country lying side by side on a football field. That's a lot, right? And every single one of them is searching for a Christmas story to run in that all-important holiday issue. Even the radio and TV are looking for things like that. Newsletters, websites, town criers, you name it. It's a way of making yourself some real money. A thought that united everyone in the class.
<v Playing Teacher>如果这听起来好得令人难以置信，那就再想想。想象一下这个国家所有的杂志和报纸并排铺在足球场上。那很多，对吧？它们每一家都在寻找一个圣诞故事来刊登在那个极其重要的节日刊号上。甚至广播和电视也在寻找这样的内容。通讯、网站、城镇公告员，凡你能想到的。这是一种让自己真正赚到钱的方式。这个想法让班上的每个人都团结了起来。

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:24.300
<v Playing Woman 1>How much money are we talking about? Can I get an advance? I want some boots.
<v Playing Woman 1>我们要赚多少钱？能预付稿费吗？我想买双靴子。

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.110
<v Playing Coach>Can the money be spent on lodging?
<v Playing Coach>钱可以用来支付住宿费吗？

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:31.520
<v Playing Teacher>The money can be spent on anything you want, lodging, boots, gas, even stamps.
<v Playing Teacher>钱可以花在任何你想花的地方，住宿、靴子、汽油，甚至是邮票。

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.780
<v Playing Irish Student>What about drink and games of chance?
<v Playing Irish Student>那酒水和博彩呢？

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:41.880
<v Playing Teacher>That too. But first, you've got to sell your story. Which means you'll have to know your audience.
<v Playing Teacher>也可以。但首先，你得把你的故事卖出去。这意味着你必须了解你的读者。

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:44.090
<v Class>[GRUMBLING AND COMPLAINING SOUNDS]
<v Class>[抱怨和不满的声音]

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:56.910
<v Playing Teacher>People, listen, listen. If you spent last Christmas, I don't know, coaching midget league basketball or designing a patio, chances are, the editors over at Cat Fancy or Chocolatier aren't going to be too interested.
<v Playing Teacher>各位，听着，听着。如果你去年圣诞节都在忙着，我不知道，训练小联盟篮球或设计露台，那么《猫咪迷》或《巧克力商》的编辑们可能不会太感兴趣。

00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:03.320
<v Playing Coach>But can't you spend the holidays coaching midget league, and like cats, and chocolate? I do.
<v Playing Coach>但不能既在假期训练小联盟，又喜欢猫和巧克力吗？我就喜欢。

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:19.960
<v Playing Teacher>No. Of course, you can do both. But, the chances are, your one line, I like cats, won't be enough for an editor who's audience is made up of people who more than like cats. For these people, the cat or the chocolate, needs to be the focus of the whole, holiday story.
<v Playing Teacher>不。当然，你可以两者兼顾。但是，很可能你那句“我喜欢猫”不足以打动编辑，因为他们的读者群是由比“喜欢猫”更进一步的人组成的。对于这些人来说，猫或巧克力需要成为整个节日故事的核心。

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:23.850
<v Playing Woman 1>What if the magazine is for people who love not liking anything?
<v Playing Woman 1>如果杂志是给那些“什么都不喜欢”的人看的呢？

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:25.920
<v Playing Teacher>Then give them my number right away.
<v Playing Teacher>那立刻把我的号码给他们。

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.200
<v Class>[LAUGHTER]
<v Class>[笑声]

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:49.890
<v Playing Teacher>No, seriously, the question's a good one. This young person seems to know what the magazine wants, and that's the first step. If you want to be published, you have to find a magazine and really stake it out. Study it under a good, strong lamp. The fine print, even the advertisements will give you an indication of who these people are and how the editors try to reach them. Yes?
<v Playing Teacher>不，说真的，这个问题问得很好。这位年轻人似乎知道杂志想要什么，这是第一步。如果你想出版，你必须找到一份杂志并真正地深耕它。在明亮的灯光下研究它。细则，甚至广告都能让你看出这些人是谁，以及编辑们是如何试图触达他们的。有哪位有疑问吗？

00:09:49.890 --> 00:10:06.800
<v Playing Southern Student>I once made a strong lamp out of a coconut husk my mother's brother's cousin Colonel Jeremy Hackford found on the beach at Topsoil Island. The husk was tattered and sunburnt, resembling the head of one of grandmother's servants. A moon-faced child by the name of--
<v Playing Southern Student>我曾经用我母亲的兄弟的表弟杰里米·哈克福德上校在表土岛海滩上发现的椰子壳做了一盏强力灯。那椰子壳又破又被晒焦了，看起来很像祖母的一个仆人的头。那个叫……的月亮脸孩子。

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:24.630
<v Playing Teacher>Great. So, I want each and every one of you to do that. Take a magazine and make it your own personal project. At the end of this course, I expect every single one of you to hold a contract in your hands. Let me rephrase that. If any of you don't want to publish a holiday story, I'm afraid I can't help you.
<v Playing Teacher>太棒了。所以，我希望你们每个人都这样做。拿一本杂志，把它作为你们个人的专项课题。课程结束时，我期望你们每个人手里都拿着一份合同。让我换个说法。如果你们当中有人不想出版节日故事，恐怕我帮不了你。

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:54.660
<v Playing Woman 1>I'd really love to be published, especially around the holidays. I'd take that money, the paper clutched tight, like a prize, like a ticket. And I'd use it to fly, like a soft prayer-carpet. I'd mount it and work our magic out of here, out of the window, and over the stained quilt of this life, this town, where every stitch is a fence, a barrier, a landscape, embroidered with no. A land sewn, not to comfort, but to smother. I'd take that carpet--
<v Playing Woman 1>我真的非常想出版，特别是在节日期间。我会拿着那笔钱，紧紧攥在手里，像个奖品，像张入场券。我会用它飞翔，像一块柔软的祈祷地毯。我会骑上它，带着我们的魔法离开这里，穿出窗户，飞过这沉重的人生、这个小镇那被打满补丁的被子。这里的每一道针脚都是一道栅栏，一道屏障，一片绣着“不”字的山水。这是一片被缝合的土地，不是为了慰藉，而是为了窒息。我会带着那块地毯——

00:10:54.660 --> 00:11:01.600
<v Playing Irish Student>What if you'd really like to be published, but certain people think that your dead and it's better off to keep it that way?
<v Playing Irish Student>如果一个人真的想出版，但某些人认为你已经死了，而且最好保持这种状态呢？

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:07.920
<v Playing Coach>What if someone found a stray kitten in the locker room while coaching midget league and eating chocolate? Can I send it to three magazines?
<v Playing Coach>如果有人在训练小联盟时在更衣室里发现了一只流浪猫，同时还在吃巧克力呢？我可以把它发给三家杂志吗？

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.650
<v Playing Woman 1>Is it all right to turn in a manuscript written in blood or semen?
<v Playing Woman 1>用血或精液写的手稿可以投递吗？

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:18.020
<v Playing Teacher>Typed, double spaced, unless it's written for one of the many handwriting analysis or calligraphy magazines.
<v Playing Teacher>打印，双倍行距，除非是为众多笔迹分析或书法杂志撰写的。

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:18.970
<v Playing Woman 1>Do 'zines count?
<v Playing Woman 1>“粉丝杂志”算吗？

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:23.150
<v Playing Irish Student>Is justifiable political manslaughter acceptable in a Christmas story?
<v Playing Irish Student>圣诞故事里可以包含合理的政治性过失杀人吗？

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:54.110
<v Playing Gene Larkin>It was a great class. Everyone was really into it. And the teacher, he had this way of firing us up and cracking the type of whip you knew wouldn't break the surface of your skin, no matter how close you got to the many barbed tips. Like a lot of us, I love to write but often have to wait for an office party or a rowdy Presbyterian funeral for the mood to hit me. The teacher though, he explained how those same feelings of jubilation or sorrow can be reproduced through drugs and alcohol or tossing a hard, cold body into a shallow grave.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>那真是一门很棒的课。每个人都全身心投入。而那位老师，他有一种激发我们热情的方式，他挥舞的那种鞭子，你知道无论你离那些倒钩尖端多近，它都不会划破你的皮肤。像我们很多人一样，我喜欢写作，但通常不得不等到办公室派对或一场吵闹的长老会葬礼才能产生灵感。然而，老师解释了如何通过药物、酒精，或者把一具冰冷的尸体扔进浅坑里，来复制那种狂喜或悲伤的感受。

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:59.820
<v Playing Irish Student>The grave part, it's like you reached in and pulled the thoughts out of my very heart.
<v Playing Irish Student>关于坟墓那部分，感觉就像你直接伸手掏出了我内心深处的想法。

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:06.710
<v Playing Coach>Or the whiff of a sneaker yanked off the foot of a husky 13-year-old with a sprained ankle and a 39-point game average?
<v Playing Coach>或者是脱掉一个有着扭伤脚踝、场均得分39分的壮硕13岁少年的运动鞋时那一阵臭味？

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:08.710
<v Class>[MURMURS OF DISAGREEMENT AND CONFUSION]
<v Class>[嘈杂的反对和困惑声]

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:14.480
<v Playing Teacher>People, people, what I'm trying to say, is that we're all people.
<v Playing Teacher>各位，各位，我想说的是，我们都是人。

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:22.030
<v Playing Gene Larkin>He was so smart that way, always reminding us that we were all people, always taking us that one step further.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>他在这方面非常聪明，总是提醒我们大家都是人，总是带着我们更进一步。

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:30.310
<v Playing Teacher>All right people, you've got your magazines. Now, let's start thinking about the stories. Christmas people, what does that bring to mind. Yvonne?
<v Playing Teacher>好了各位，你们已经选好杂志了。现在，让我们开始构思故事吧。圣诞节的人，这让你想到了什么？伊冯娜？

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:55.870
<v Playing Woman 1>Christmas. All right. Christmas, the holidays. A really small knapsack filled with nail polish. It's lying there on the futon. I see something. It's something corduroy crumbled up and next to the TV. And there's a hair brush, my hair brush, used to prop open the window. It's cold outside and raining. And I can hear my roommate crying in the next room.
<v Playing Woman 1>圣诞节。好吧。圣诞节，假期。一个装满指甲油的小背包。它躺在榻榻米上。我看到一样东西。那是某种褶皱的灯芯绒放在电视机旁边。还有一个发刷，我的发刷，用来撑开窗户。外面很冷，还在下雨。我能听到隔壁房间里我的室友在哭。

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:56.860
<v Class>[LAUGHTER]
<v Class>[笑声]

00:12:56.860 --> 00:13:13.710
<v Playing Coach>So it's minus 12 degrees outside, and I'm at an out-of-town game coaching the Lady Diamondbacks. Can you believe it? You couldn't find a sweeter, more talented group of girls. And every one of them was 100% drug free. I made sure of that baby.
<v Playing Coach>当时外面零下12度，我正在外地教练“戴蒙德巴克小姐队”。你能相信吗？你找不到比这群女孩更可爱、更有天赋的团队了。她们每个人都百分之百不碰违禁品。我保证这一点，宝贝。

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:32.030
<v Playing Irish Student>She turned to me on last time saying, don't do it Jerry Maguire. You were born for better things. Drink up now son. It's Christmas time, and we've got an early morning's Mass tomorrow. [CHOKING BACK TEARS] That was the last time I saw her.
<v Playing Irish Student>她最后一次转向我说，别做那傻事，杰里·麦奎尔。你生来就是做大事的人。喝吧，孩子。现在是圣诞节了，我们明天还有清晨的弥撒。[哽咽]那是我最后一次见到她。

00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:36.620
<v Playing Teacher>I like that, corduroy and the Lady Diamondbacks. What else?
<v Playing Teacher>我喜欢那个，灯芯绒和戴蒙德巴克小姐队。还有别的吗？

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:50.350
<v Playing Southern Student>The sky was cloudless, the color of a burnished silver platter on that day when daddy and I set out with our aging spaniel Curtis in search of, what we considered to be, the perfect Christmas tree. A magnificent--
<v Playing Southern Student>天空晴朗无云，像一个抛光的银盘，那天我和爸爸带着我们上了年纪的西班牙猎犬柯蒂斯出发，去寻找我们认为完美的圣诞树。一棵壮丽的——

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:55.190
<v Playing Teacher>Conflict, good. Every story needs it. You can sit down now, sister.
<v Playing Teacher>冲突，很好。每个故事都需要冲突。你可以坐下了，姐妹。

00:13:55.190 --> 00:14:31.910
<v Playing Gene Larkin>The class started in early September, so as to allow us to finish our stories early enough to allow magazines, what they call, their lead-time. Back-breaking unions force these publishers to be ready weeks, sometimes months in advance. To us, Christmas falls somewhere towards the end of December. But to these editors, that holiday has already come and gone. They're working on Valentine's Day or President's Day, or even Saint Patrick's by that time. It was hard to think about the holidays when, technically, it was late summer, and people were still dressed in shorts and halter tops. But the teacher, he did his best to get us into the spirit.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>课程从九月初开始，以便让我们能足够早地完成故事，给杂志留出他们所谓的“交稿周期”。繁重的工会要求这些出版商提前几周甚至几个月准备好内容。对我们来说，圣诞节发生在十二月底。但对于这些编辑来说，节日已经过去了。他们那时正在做情人节、总统日，甚至是圣帕特里克节的策划。当天气还是夏末，人们还穿着短裤和吊带背心时，要考虑节日真的很困难。但老师尽力让我们进入状态。

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:37.300
<v Playing Teacher>All right people, I've got this fragrant, eight foot spruce. What do you say, we trim it?
<v Playing Teacher>各位，我有一棵芳香的八英尺高的云杉。你们说，我们要装饰它吗？

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:38.960
<v Playing Coach>Now, in the daylight?
<v Playing Coach>现在，在大白天？

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:40.620
<v Playing Teacher>We can mask the window.
<v Playing Teacher>我们可以把窗户遮住。

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:45.410
<v Playing Coach>Then how will I know when my nephew's come to pick me up? You know I have to leave early on Thursdays.
<v Playing Coach>那我怎么知道我侄子什么时候来接我？你知道我周四必须早点走。

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:52.450
<v Playing Irish Student>If you don't mind, I think I'll wait outside in the student pub. There's something about trees inside that makes me nervous.
<v Playing Irish Student>如果你不介意的话，我想在学生酒吧外面等。树放在室内总让我感到紧张。

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:55.550
<v Playing Woman 1>Don't ornaments carry a lot of baggage?
<v Playing Woman 1>装饰品难道不带有太多的负担吗？

00:14:55.550 --> 00:15:02.070
<v Playing Teacher>They might, they very well might. But I'm ready to face my demons. How about you?
<v Playing Teacher>也许会，很有可能。但我准备面对我的心魔了。你呢？

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:40.050
<v Playing Gene Larkin>It wasn't easy or fun, but we did it. We did it to remind ourselves what we were writing about. Outside the building, the trees were green and leafy. But in this room, it was Christmas, even to the several Jews and heretics. A few people stepped outside to smoke or make phone calls. And in a way, I think they really missed out. Maybe they could just hold Christmas inside their heads by re-reading old catalogs or paying bills. But for most of us, these little exercises really helped. The teacher had us start each class with a carol.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>这并不容易，也不有趣，但我们做到了。我们这样做是为了提醒自己我们在写些什么。大楼外，树木依然郁郁葱葱。但在教室里，已经是圣诞节了，即便是对那些犹太人和异教徒来说也是如此。有些人走出教室去抽烟或打电话。某种程度上，我觉得他们真的错过了。也许他们只能通过重读旧目录或付账单在脑海中保持圣诞氛围。但对于我们大多数人来说，这些练习真的很有帮助。老师让我们每节课都以颂歌开始。

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:47.200
<v Class>[HUMMING  CHRISTMAS CAROLS]
<v Class>[哼唱圣诞颂歌]

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:58.310
<v Playing Gene Larkin>And we all shaved ice, wrapped empty boxes as gifts, and sent fake, funny checks to charities. One day, he even brought chestnuts into the class and roasted them, right there, on a small, charcoal grill.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>我们都刮冰屑，把空盒子当作礼物包装起来，并向慈善机构寄去有趣的假支票。有一天，他甚至带了一些栗子到教室里，就在那个小木炭烤架上烤了起来。

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:01.330
<v Class>[COUGHING, CHOKING]
<v Class>[咳嗽、呛声]

00:16:01.330 --> 00:16:08.340
<v Playing Gene Larkin>He made a chart of what each magazine paid per word and read us a few examples of their previous holiday stories.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>他做了一张图表，列出了每本杂志每字的稿费，并给我们读了几个他们以前节日故事的例子。

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:25.540
<v Playing Teacher>It was grueling work, but I did it. I'd received the doe late on Christmas Eve, and had her fully mounted by the next morning, just in time to make one little girl's dreams come true. That's "Doe for a Deer," from the December '94 issue of Mounted Game Monthly .
<v Playing Teacher>工作很艰苦，但我完成了。我在平安夜很晚才收到那头母鹿，到第二天早上就把它完全制成了标本，正好赶上让一个小女孩梦想成真。这就是刊登在94年12月《标本狩猎月刊》上的《母鹿的故事》。

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:27.910
<v Playing Coach>That was very beautiful.
<v Playing Coach>那太美了。

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:33.080
<v Playing Teacher>Yes, it was. So how many words did we have there, word count, people?
<v Playing Teacher>是的，确实很美。那么我们这里有多少字，数一下，各位？

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:35.640
<v Playing Woman 1>He stretched the skin on a pallet.
<v Playing Woman 1>他在托盘上拉伸了皮肤。

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:44.490
<v Playing Irish Student>According to my calculations, and I got a little bit bleary at the sad parts, I'd say that that story clocked in at about 945 words.
<v Playing Irish Student>根据我的计算，而且在悲伤的部分我还有点眼花，我觉得那个故事大约有945字。

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:47.710
<v Playing Teacher>All right. And this particular magazine pays how much per word?
<v Playing Teacher>好了。那么这本特定的杂志每字付多少钱？

00:16:47.710 --> 00:16:49.340
<v Playing Coach>$0.55 per word.
<v Playing Coach>每字0.55美元。

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:56.780
<v Playing Teacher>So that amounts to what? That's $472 and some change for our friend, Mr. Carlton Burke, from mounted game monthly. Yes?
<v Playing Teacher>那总共是多少？我们这位来自《标本狩猎月刊》的朋友卡尔顿·伯克先生赚了472美元多一点。有疑问吗？

00:16:56.780 --> 00:16:59.820
<v Playing Woman 1>But this class cost me $600.
<v Playing Woman 1>但这门课花了我600美元。

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:02.340
<v Playing Irish Student>Hey, that's right.
<v Playing Irish Student>嘿，你说得对。

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:03.280
<v Playing Woman 1>This is a rip-off.
<v Playing Woman 1>这简直是坑人。

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:04.710
<v Playing Gene Larkin>You just can't win.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>你就是赢不了。

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:09.590
<v Playing Coach>The whole system is rigged. Isn't there a way this Carlton person could've made more money?
<v Playing Coach>整个系统都是操纵好的。难道这个卡尔顿就不能赚更多钱吗？

00:17:09.590 --> 00:17:16.620
<v Playing Teacher>Yes, there is. He could've sold it to a better magazine or padded it with adjectives and adverbs.
<v Playing Teacher>可以的。他可以把它卖给更好的杂志，或者用形容词和副词来充实它。

00:17:16.620 --> 00:17:23.940
<v Playing Gene Larkin>He went back through the story, demonstrating how the author might have made extra money by employing a more fragrant and descriptive vocabulary.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>他回到故事中，演示了作者如何通过运用更生动、更具描述性的词汇来赚取额外收入。

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:28.990
<v Playing Teacher>Take this one word, needle. What else could he have called it? Yes?
<v Playing Teacher>拿这个词来说，针。他还可以称它为什么？哪位有想法？

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:38.090
<v Playing Irish Student>A sharp, pin-like device used for piercing holiday fabric or the flesh of a doe shot and mounted in order to serve as a gift for the holidays?
<v Playing Irish Student>一种尖锐的、类似针的装置，用于刺穿节日布料，或刺穿为节日礼物而射杀并制成标本的母鹿的皮肉？

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:52.060
<v Playing Teacher>That's terrific. See class, he's taken one word and stretched it into a 29 word sentence, with the added value of $19.95. And he's related it all to the holidays. Yes?
<v Playing Teacher>太棒了。大家看，他把一个词扩展成了一个29个字的句子，还额外赋予了19.95美元的价值。而且他把这一切都和节日联系在了一起。还有吗？

00:17:52.060 --> 00:18:00.780
<v Playing Southern Student>I remember, one time, the fields behind granddaddy's smoke house were flooded. And the starlings circled and quested, crisscrossing like so many fleeting--
<v Playing Southern Student>我记得有一次，爷爷熏肉房后面的田地被淹了。椋鸟盘旋着，寻找着，像许多飞逝的——一样来回穿梭

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:14.860
<v Playing Teacher>What do you know. Listen people, we've only got three weeks left until Halloween. Let's do a little exercise. How many of you remember what it felt like to rise at 5:00 AM and shovel the driveway so that your visiting relatives would have a place to pitch their Christmas tent?
<v Playing Teacher>真有你的。听着，伙计们，离万圣节只剩三个星期了。我们做一个小练习。你们中有多少人记得清晨5点起床铲车道的感觉，只为了让来访的亲戚有个地方搭他们的圣诞帐篷？

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:20.060
<v Playing Coach>I do. It was dark then. I had to use a lantern, and it smelled like gas.
<v Playing Coach>我记得。那时天还没亮。我得用盏灯，而且闻起来有股煤气味。

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:32.230
<v Playing Irish Student>Huh, the O'Shaughnessys on my mother's side, they thought they were all too good for tents. Talk about hard work. I'd be up at 3:00 in the morning, digging their lean-tos and huts out of the cold ground, without even gloves to project me.
<v Playing Irish Student>哈，我母亲那边的奥肖内西一家，他们觉得住帐篷太掉价了。说起辛苦，我得在凌晨3点起床，从冰冷的地面里把他们的简易房和棚屋挖出来，连双手套都没有，也没人保护我。

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:36.560
<v Playing Gene Larkin>Everything the teacher said opened a door or triggered a memory of some kind.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>老师说的每一句话都打开了一扇门，或者触发了某种记忆。

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:40.920
<v Playing Teacher>How many of you have accidentally blinded a family member with a tree limb or an icicle?
<v Playing Teacher>你们中有多少人曾经不小心用树枝或冰柱弄伤过家人的眼睛？

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:41.540
<v Playing Gene Larkin>I have.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>我有过。

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:43.330
<v Playing Southern Student>I'd forgotten all about that.
<v Playing Southern Student>我差点都忘了这件事了。

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:44.740
<v Playing Irish Student>Guilty as charged.
<v Playing Irish Student>我也一样，有罪。

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:46.330
<v Playing Woman 1>Talk about a Christmas.
<v Playing Woman 1>这就是所谓的圣诞节啊。

00:18:46.330 --> 00:18:48.050
<v Playing Coach>Just a team member and a benched one.
<v Playing Coach>只是一个队里的成员，还有一个在板凳上。

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:53.900
<v Playing Teacher>Remember people, you're all just types. Now, let's get out there and make some money.
<v Playing Teacher>记住各位，你们都只是类型。现在，出去赚点钱吧。

00:18:53.900 --> 00:19:21.370
<v Playing Gene Larkin>He never asked what we were working on, never assigned us any homework, or shared any of the holiday stories he had written. He lead by example and taught on the sheer strength of his character. Over the weeks, I came to think of him and the other students as family. They started getting on my nerves in little ways. But I couldn't hate them, because, as time passed, we grew to resemble one another, growing out our nails and wearing identical wind-breakers.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>他从不问我们在做什么，从不布置家庭作业，也从不分享他写过的节日故事。他以身作则，凭借纯粹的人格力量进行教导。几周下来，我开始把他和其他学生当作家人。他们开始在一些小事上让我感到厌烦。但我无法讨厌他们，因为随着时间的推移，我们长得越来越像，留长了指甲，穿着一模一样的防风夹克。

00:19:21.370 --> 00:19:47.770
<v Playing Gene Larkin>The teacher noticed the change and discouraged us from talking about our stories, for fear of inciting the sort of jealousy that often results in bloodshed or small fires. On the last day of class, we each arrived carrying our completed holiday story, ready for submission, each including a stamped, self-addressed envelope. And then we celebrated with punch and carols, laughing and reminiscing until the sun set on that late October afternoon.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>老师注意到了这种变化，并劝阻我们讨论彼此的故事，以免引发那种常导致流血或小规模火灾的嫉妒。在课程的最后一天，我们每个人都带着完成的节日故事来到这里，准备提交，每人都附上了一个贴好邮票的回邮信封。然后，我们喝着潘趣酒，唱着颂歌，欢笑着，回忆着，直到十月那个午后的太阳落下。

00:19:47.770 --> 00:20:12.380
<v Playing Gene Larkin>Oh, there were the usual pledges to stay in touch. Numbers were exchanged and casts were signed, but I think we knew in our hearts that this would be our last get together. Having become professional holiday writers, we were no longer friends but rivals. Wishing each other the best, while quietly hoping for the worse. I moved on with my new life.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>哦，大家当然都承诺保持联系。交换了号码，在石膏上签了名，但我认为我们心里都很清楚，这将是我们最后一次聚会。成为了职业节日作家后，我们不再是朋友，而是竞争对手。表面上祝对方一切顺利，心里却默默盼着对方倒霉。我继续着我的新生活。

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:38.740
<v Playing Gene Larkin>I spent a few weeks getting re-acquainted with myself, and then I took a job at one of the several petting mangers my town sets up each year during the holiday season. It was right after Thanksgiving. And I had a bit to drink. Maybe I was showing off. Maybe I was just a little too happy to be me. I drove my go-kart into the petting manger. And some of these animals, the camels and so forth, they spook a little too easy.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>我花了几个星期的时间重新找回自我，然后我在镇上每年节日期间设立的几个动物喂养槽之一找到了一份工作。那是感恩节刚过的时候。我喝了点酒。也许我是在炫耀。也许我只是因为做自己而太高兴了。我把卡丁车开进了喂养槽。而这些动物中的一些，比如骆驼之类的，它们太容易受惊了。

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:45.160
<v Playing Gene Larkin>The next thing I knew, it was two weeks later. And I was flat on my back in the hospital, waking from a coma.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>等我再有意识的时候，已经是两周后了。我平躺在医院里，从昏迷中醒来。

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:49.230
<v Nurse>Oh, doctor, the man in 26B just woke up from his coma.
<v Nurse>哦，医生，26B病房的病人刚刚从昏迷中醒来了。

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:51.740
<v Doctor>Oh, good. You can send him home now.
<v Doctor>哦，好极了。你现在可以让他出院了。

00:20:51.740 --> 00:21:22.180
<v Playing Gene Larkin>I was discharged and returned to my house without walls, with a few scratches, a significant headache, and a prescription for a couple of salves they thought might heal a few of my bed sores. It was December, and I was in the drug store having my prescription filled. There was a stomach virus going around, and the pharmacist's line was 30, 40 people long. I was standing back near the magazine rack, thumbing through the current issue of Cotton Belt Lifestyles , when I read what sounded like a familiar voice.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>我出院回到了那个四面漏风的家，带着几道擦伤，严重的头痛，还有一张处方，上面写着他们认为可以治愈我几处褥疮的药膏。那是十二月，我在药店配药。当时正流行肠胃病毒，药剂师的队伍排了30、40个人长。我站在杂志架旁，翻阅着最新一期的《棉花带生活方式》，这时我读到了听起来很熟悉的声音。

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:51.280
<v Playing Southern Student>We'd gone to bed late that Christmas Eve. Taka, Soil, Prescott, and I lay huddled in our beds, rowdy as newborn lambs vying for a swollen teat. And while I have no recollection of the exact moment, sleep miraculously overcame us at some point during that long and restless night. The world spunt on it's frosty axis, and morning arrived. The brilliant sun trumpeting the arrival of this day when children celebrate the birth of a king.
<v Playing Southern Student>那个平安夜我们睡得很晚。塔卡、索伊尔、普雷斯科特和我蜷缩在床上，像争抢母乳的新生羔羊一样吵闹。虽然我不记得具体的时间，但在那个漫长而不安的夜晚，睡眠奇迹般地战胜了我们。世界在它寒霜的轴心上旋转，清晨到来了。灿烂的太阳宣告着这个孩子们庆祝国王诞生的日子。

00:21:51.280 --> 00:21:53.120
<v Playing Gene Larkin>I started skimming ahead.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>我开始跳读。

00:21:53.120 --> 00:22:33.290
<v Playing Southern Student>That was the year each child received a slingshot. Oh, it wasn't much, just a rubber band attached to a wishbone from one of old man Chesterton's tom turkeys. But oh how those bones were polished to a sheen. The rubber band attached with pride and precision. Now, I can catch us a squirrel for supper, said Prescott, the youngest. And we all laughed, as children are wont to do. It's a sound that haunts me still, stops me in my tracks on this cold winter day, as Prescott, so young and eager to please, would not live to see another Christmas. February is the coolest month.
<v Playing Southern Student>那是每个孩子都收到弹弓的一年。哦，那算不上什么，只是绑在切斯特顿老头的一只火鸡叉骨上的橡皮筋。但那些骨头被擦得锃亮。橡皮筋被自豪而精确地绑上去。现在，我能抓只松鼠当晚饭了，最小的普雷斯科特说。我们都笑了，就像孩子们常做的那样。那声音至今仍萦绕在我心头，在这个寒冷的冬日让我驻足，因为普雷斯科特，如此年轻又渴望取悦他人，却没能活到下一个圣诞节。二月是一年中最冷的月份。

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:37.400
<v Playing Gene Larkin>I picked up another magazine, then another, grabbing them randomly off the shelves.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>我拿起另一本杂志，又一本，随机地从书架上抓取。

00:22:37.400 --> 00:23:00.040
<v Playing Coach>Basketball season had started early that year. And with my starting forward out with a torn meniscus, it didn't seem my Christmas was going to merry. I laced up my street shoes and stared out the window into the parking lot. We were expecting a minor dusting, but it was coming down hard, the flakes the size of those Styrofoam pellets they use to package trophies. Trophies, better put that thought out of your mind, baby, I said to myself.
<v Playing Coach>那年的篮球赛季开始得很早。由于我的首发前锋半月板撕裂，看来我的圣诞节不会快乐了。我系好运动鞋，透过窗户盯着停车场。我们预料会有少量降雪，但雪下得很大，雪花大得像那些用来包装奖杯的泡沫颗粒。奖杯，最好把这种想法从脑子里赶走，宝贝，我对自己说。

00:23:00.040 --> 00:23:25.110
<v Playing Coach>It's three hours before the playoff. And I'm stuck in Manitoba, spoon feeding cranberry juice cocktail to my star point-guard Shanethra, who's got a stomach virus, and all the trimmings, and a fever of 102. The bus has moved on, and I'm there with Shanethra, who's flat on her back and her best friend Stephanie Twospears, who's sweating many, many rivers. I'm in a Holiday Inn, and the room is hot and smells of powder, modeling glue, and teak. There was a wreath on the door, and nothing good was on TV.
<v Playing Coach>离季后赛还有三个小时。我被困在曼尼托巴省，给患了肠胃病毒的明星控球后卫沙内斯拉喂蔓越莓汁，还有所有的装饰物，她发着102度的高烧。大巴已经开走了，我留在那儿陪着平躺着的沙内斯拉，还有她那汗流浃背的好友斯蒂芬妮·双矛。我在一家假日酒店，房间里很热，闻起来有粉末、模型胶和柚木的味道。门上挂着花环，电视上也没什么好看的。

00:23:25.110 --> 00:24:01.250
<v Playing Woman 1>She wanted to shake it, not just the tree or the jagged ice daggers dripping from the roof, but the whole damn thing. She wanted to shake it like Stolly shook his mane of dark hair, the color of night, the color of ravens, of crows calling out in their hoarse whispers. Stolly shaking his head while playing the bass, that night before Christmas, silent night, the holy night. They spiked two grams of horse and ran wild, ran like the wind that brings up from a fire. She wanted to shake it.
<v Playing Woman 1>她想摇晃它，不是摇晃那棵树或屋檐下滴下的参差不齐的冰锥，而是摇晃这整个该死的东西。她想摇晃它，就像斯托利摇晃他那头如夜色般乌黑、如乌鸦般深邃、如乌鸦在嘶哑耳语中呼唤的长发。斯托利在圣诞前夜弹着贝斯时摇头，平安夜，神圣的夜。他们给马注射了两克海洛因，像从火中升起的风一样狂奔。她想摇晃它。

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:18.990
<v Playing Irish Student>The wreath swayed as the police pounded on the other side of the door. Go away and leave us in peace, my mother shouted. I don't know nothing about the disappearance of the O'Shaughnessy brothers. No one here does. No go away with ya. She motioned to me with her cane, and, I remember, she had a wee' bit o' tinsel on the slope of her shoulder.
<v Playing Irish Student>当警察在门外敲门时，花环摇晃了一下。走开，让我们安静点，我母亲喊道。我不知道奥肖内西兄弟失踪的事。这里没人知道。快走吧。她用手杖向我示意，我记得，她的肩膀上还挂着一点点闪亮的箔条。

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:48.080
<v Playing Irish Student>High-tail it out the back door and get word to Paddy McShane, she whispered. Go on now, go. Which I did. I sloped out the back way, and ran with all my might. I ran past O'Brians Pub and McNamara's Alehouse, past O'Malley's Tavern and Feeny's Roadhouse. I passed Father McGonegal coming out of the church, and I shouted, for the love of Christmas, who's seen Paddy McShane?
<v Playing Irish Student>从后门溜走，去给帕迪·麦克沙恩送信，她低声说。快走，快。我照做了。我从后路溜走，用尽全力奔跑。我跑过奥布莱恩酒吧和麦克纳马拉啤酒屋，跑过奥马利酒馆和费尼路边餐馆。我经过从教堂出来的麦格内格尔神父身边，我喊道，看在圣诞节的份上，谁见到帕迪·麦克沙恩了？

00:24:48.080 --> 00:25:06.560
<v Playing Gene Larkin>It made me smile to think of my friends and that wonderful preseason Christmas we'd spent getting to know one another. It seemed they  had turned in their stories and had fulfilled their dreams. Me, I had never gotten around to writing my story, much less mailing it. My envelope had been blank.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>想到我的朋友们以及我们在那个美妙的季前圣诞节里彼此了解，我露出了微笑。看来他们已经提交了故事并实现了他们的梦想。而我，从没写过故事，更没寄出。我的信封是空的。

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:26.510
<v Playing Gene Larkin>I turned to the woman behind me and told he all about it. Then she turned to the person behind her, and so forth, and so on, until it made its way to the radio producer with stomach cramps and a deadline for his last-minute Christmas story. So I guess I did find my audience after all, didn't I. Good night all, and God bless.
<v Playing Gene Larkin>我转过身，把这一切都告诉了身后那个女人。然后她转过身告诉她身后的人，以此类推，直到传到了那位患有胃痉挛、还要赶在圣诞节前夜完成故事的电台制作人耳中。所以我猜我最终还是找到了我的观众，不是吗。大家晚安，上帝保佑。

00:25:26.510 --> 00:27:54.130
