<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[平安夜]

00:27:54.130 --> 00:28:11.880
<v Ira Glass>Little music from the Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church from Chicago's South Side. We'll be hearing music from them throughout the hour, and we're going to have a visit with them at the end of the hour. Our play by David Sedaris and the Pine Tree Gang, exclusively here on Your Radio Playhouse radio program.
<v Ira Glass>这是来自芝加哥南区信仰会幕浸信会教堂的音乐。我们将在整点节目中听到他们的音乐，节目结束时我们还将拜访他们。这是由大卫·塞达里斯和松树帮带来的剧目，仅在您的广播剧场广播节目中播出。

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:38.220
<v Ira Glass>Coming up next, in our little cavalcade of Christmas stories, is a story from Beau O'Riley. Beau's a local playwright and musician, longtime member of the band Maestro Subgum and the Whole. And Christmas brings up such different feelings and memories for each person. His story is a story from his childhood living outside Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，在我们的小型圣诞故事巡礼中，是一个来自博·奥莱利的故事。博是一位当地的剧作家和音乐家，也是乐队“大师苏布冈和全体成员”的长期成员。圣诞节为每个人带来了如此不同的感受和回忆。他的故事是他童年住在芝加哥郊外的故事。

00:28:38.220 --> 00:28:41.340
<v Beau O'reilly>I lost my entire thumbnail closing a classroom window.
<v Beau O'reilly>我在关教室窗户时弄掉了整个大拇指指甲。

00:28:41.340 --> 00:29:08.310
<v Ira Glass>I'm just going to start that again. We're having a cuing problem here. You can see that we are spooked by the Ghost of Christmas Present here in this program so far. It's been such a nerve wracking thing getting the program on the air, with everybody going away for the holidays, and us wanting to make the show extra special with all sorts of extra special new pieces. So we're just going to move back. You see, we're just going to have Ghost of Christmas Past here. We just going to move back into past about a minute ago and try that again.
<v Ira Glass>我要重新开始。我们在提示上出了点问题。你可以看出我们在这节目中被现在的圣诞幽灵吓到了。为了把节目播出，我们真是伤透了脑筋，大家都去过节了，而我们想用各种特别的新作品让节目格外精彩。所以我们要往回走。你看，我们这里只有过去的圣诞幽灵。我们只需回到一分钟前，再试一次。

00:29:08.310 --> 00:29:33.310
<v Beau O'reilly>Two weeks before Christmas in 1966, I lost my entire thumbnail closing a classroom window as part of the Sister Edwardine seventh graders helped the school close their windows program. I'd been assigned to close them all up every day. And they were huge, swinging, picture-window type windows that demanded a seventh graders brooding aggressiveness to shut them firmly.
<v Beau O'reilly>1966年圣诞节前两周，我在协助爱德华丁修女七年级学生开展的学校关窗计划中，关教室窗户时弄掉了整个大拇指指甲。我每天都被指派去把它们都关上。那是一些巨大的、旋转的、落地窗式的窗户，需要七年级学生那种阴郁的攻击性才能紧紧地关上。

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:55.320
<v Beau O'reilly>It was warm that day, unseasonable, and all the windows were wide open. And the other boy just let fly, really slamming it. I was dreaming out the window when the metal rim of it caught the top of my thumbnail, and the whole nail just sprang off. There was blood and nerve endings skit-scatting out of my thumb, hot pain.
<v Beau O'reilly>那天天气很暖和，反常的暖和，所有的窗户都大开着。另一个男孩刚才用力一甩，猛地关上了窗户。我当时正对着窗外做白日梦，窗户的金属边缘钩住了我的大拇指指甲盖，整个指甲就这么弹飞了。血和神经末梢从我的拇指里溅了出来，那种滚烫的疼痛。

00:29:55.320 --> 00:30:23.930
<v Beau O'reilly>A week later, I was back on the playground, my thumb bound in a plastic splint. Wrapped in so much gauze that it, the thumb, looked like an upside down bowling pin. I was five nine and a half in 1966 and built like a garden hose that had been used so many times that it has lost all sense of its rubbery toughness, its definition, and hangs like a limp spiral. My body was like that, pale skin wrapped around awkward bones.
<v Beau O'reilly>一周后，我回到操场上，拇指缠着塑料夹板。裹着厚厚的纱布，看起来像一个倒扣的保龄球瓶。1966年我五英尺九英寸半，身材像一根用了太久以至于失去了橡胶韧性和轮廓、像个松垮的螺旋一样悬挂着的软管。我的身体就是那样，苍白的皮肤包裹着笨拙的骨头。

00:30:23.930 --> 00:30:54.140
<v Beau O'reilly>And that body had reached its full man-height at the end of the six grade. It would never grow another inch. This growth spurt had prompted a new attention on the playground. Suddenly, I might be of some use. Basketball was no joke on the playgrounds of my midwestern-boy-1966-hood. Basketball was the most excellent, athletically rational of games and, suddenly, I was eligible because of a quirk of genetics but still eligible.
<v Beau O'reilly>那具身体在六年级结束时就长到了成年后的最高身高。它再也不会长高一英寸了。这次生长突增引起了操场上的新关注。突然间，我可能有点用了。在我那1966年美国中西部男孩的社区操场上，篮球可不是开玩笑的。篮球是极其出色、竞技上合理的运动，而突然间，由于基因的偶然巧合，我有资格参加了。

00:30:54.140 --> 00:31:20.170
<v Beau O'reilly>Previously, my childhood had laid itself out as one continuous torture chamber of belly clenching, flush producing fear, when it came to anything like running, jumping, or slamming. And now here I was, under the basket, pounding my dribble with my left hand, the bowling pin thumb riding the ball, a stiff little Napoleon rolling into battle.
<v Beau O'reilly>以前，我的童年一直是在跑步、跳跃或扣篮等运动时，那无休止的腹部紧绷、脸红耳赤的恐惧中度过的。而现在我站在篮下，左手拍着球，保龄球瓶一样的拇指搭在球上，像个僵硬的小拿破仑滚进战场。

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:48.860
<v Beau O'reilly>John Crescent was six two. He was almost fifteen, old for our class, and the shadow of blue whiskers edged his face. He was on the other team. And I had never noticed him before, except once, when dreaming out of Sister Edwardine's classroom window. John Crescent leaving school early for some reason, stopping at the edge of the parking lot to light a cigarette while still on school property. It was a bold enough move that I noted him and noted it.
<v Beau O'reilly>约翰·克雷森六英尺二英寸。他快十五岁了，在我们班算大的，蓝色的胡茬在他的脸上蔓延。他在另一支球队。我以前从没注意过他，除了有一次，在爱德华丁修女的教室窗外做白日梦时，看到约翰·克雷森因为某种原因提前放学，停在停车场边缘吸烟，当时还在学校范围内。这举动大胆到让我注意到了他。

00:31:48.860 --> 00:32:31.110
<v Beau O'reilly>And white trash is what I noted. Where I got that phrase, I don't know. My parents, in 1966, were careful Kennedy liberals. And white trash was way too denigrating a phrase for me to have heard at home. But the phrase hung way back in my cultural awareness, shadows of trailer parks, and country music, and Brylcreem, and Twinkies three meals a day. John Crescent had all this about him. He spoke with a heavy, Tennessee, sweet-slur to his vowels. His teeth were dark and mossy. And right now, he was coming straight at me under the basket, his eyes wide after the basketball that I was pounding.
<v Beau O'reilly>而且我注意到的是“白人垃圾”。我不知道从哪听来的这个词。1966年，我的父母是谨慎的肯尼迪自由派。而“白人垃圾”这个词对于在家里听到来说太贬义了。但这个词悬在我文化意识的深处，关于拖车公园、乡村音乐、头油和一日三餐吃甜甜圈的阴影。约翰·克雷森身上就有这一切。他说话时带着浓重的田纳西口音，元音发音含糊。他的牙齿又黑又脏。现在，他正从篮下直冲我而来，双眼死死盯着我拍动的篮球。

00:32:31.110 --> 00:33:06.830
<v Beau O'reilly>I was an odd boy back then. And the meaner kids all called me odd-head. And, I think, in that moment, I was thinking about white trash culture. My odd head cocked at an angle as I considered all of white trash culture. Its angles. Did white trash families spend their Sundays like professional wrestlers, tearing up the living room? Did they chew tobacco before they reached puberty? I was thinking these thoughts and not at all protecting the basketball when John Crescent hit me with his whole body and the ball spurted away. And I hit the pavement.
<v Beau O'reilly>那时候我是一个奇怪的男孩。刻薄的孩子们都叫我“怪头”。我想，在那一刻，我正在思考白人垃圾文化。当我在思考整个白人垃圾文化及其偏见时，我的怪头歪向一边。白人垃圾家庭的周日是不是像职业摔跤手一样，把客厅拆得乱七八糟？他们在青春期前就嚼烟草吗？我正想着这些，根本没有保护篮球，这时约翰·克雷森用他的全身撞了我，球喷了出去。我撞到了水泥地上。

00:33:06.830 --> 00:33:35.310
<v Beau O'reilly>Now, I have a tendency to go all odd-headed and get day-dreamy in the midst of violence. And this dreamy quality has gotten me through more than my share of playground fights. I float back up once I get knocked down. And I floated back up this time, in time to take a return pass and to have John Crescent slam me with this whole body a second time. But this time I didn't fall. I floated to a new spot on the court, and I held onto the ball.
<v Beau O'reilly>现在，我有一种在暴力中变得“怪头怪脑”并开始做白日梦的倾向。这种梦幻般的特质让我度过了不少操场上的争斗。一旦被击倒，我会飘回来。这次我也飘了回来，刚好接住回传球，然后约翰·克雷森第二次用全身撞了我。但这次我没有倒下。我飘到了球场的另一个位置，并紧紧抱住了球。

00:33:35.310 --> 00:34:23.760
<v Beau O'reilly>And John Crescent, just caught in surprise, and shoved me halfway across the lot. But when I rose up, still dreaming, and fired a shot over John Crescent's head, making the basket, he stopped. And then John Crescent grabbed my thumb. He grabbed it hard. It still waved like an imp with a baton above my hand. And John Crescent grabbed that nailless thumb, and he squeezed it too. And the pain went up my spine, and I popped my eyes open. John Crescent cawed, and he squeezed again. And now, I didn't know John Crescent before this. I had never had a conversation with him. And here he was squeezing my poor nerve-exposed thumb, like it was the trigger and I was the gun.
<v Beau O'reilly>而约翰·克雷森，被惊呆了，把我推过了半个场地。但当我重新站起来，依然在做梦，并从约翰·克雷森头顶投篮命中时，他停住了。然后约翰·克雷森抓住了我的拇指。他抓得很用力。它在我的手上方像个拿着指挥棒的小妖精一样摇摆。约翰·克雷森抓住了那只没指甲的拇指，还捏了它。疼痛顺着我的脊椎往上爬，我的眼睛猛地睁开了。约翰·克雷森尖叫了一声，又捏了一下。我在此之前并不认识他。我从来没和他交谈过。而他在这里捏着我可怜的、神经暴露的拇指，就像它是扳机，我是枪一样。

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:55.250
<v Beau O'reilly>I hit him with my right hand. A clumsy punch that missed his chin and glanced his Adam's apple. John Crescent gulped and he stepped back. And I hit him again, this time, somewhere near the chest, with my left hand. The little Napoleon digit crying out with more pain that made my spine jump for revenge. I don't know how long I kept hitting him. Long enough for all the other playground boys to surround the two of us, to comment on my lack of boxing skills.
<v Beau O'reilly>我用右手打了他。那一拳很笨拙，没打中他的下巴，却擦过了他的喉结。约翰·克雷森吞咽了一下，退后了一步。我又打了他一下，这次是用左手，打在他的胸口附近。那个小拿破仑手指发出更多的疼痛，让我的脊椎因为报复而跳动。我不知道我打了多久。久到操场上所有的男孩都围住了我们，评论我缺乏拳击技巧。

00:34:55.250 --> 00:35:29.890
<v Beau O'reilly>And even as I was hitting John Crescent, my odd-head cocked back for a better view of the scene. I was watching the other boys watching me. I'd grown up with most of them. I'd been beaten up by most of them. I'd been mocked and teased without remorse by most of them. They weren't my friends. I happened to have a growth spurt in time for basketball season. They didn't care about me. And yet their faces all hoped and wanted, needed me, to beat on John Crescent, white trash John Crescent, who was more of an outsider than odd-headed me.
<v Beau O'reilly>即使在我打约翰·克雷森的时候，我的“怪头”也歪向一边，以便更好地观察现场。我看着其他的男孩看着我。我和他们中的大多数一起长大。我被他们中的大多数揍过。我被他们中的大多数毫不留情地嘲笑和捉弄过。他们不是我的朋友。我只是刚好在篮球赛季前经历了一次生长突增。他们并不关心我。然而他们的脸上都希望、渴望并需要我揍约翰·克雷森，那个比我这个怪头怪脑的人更像外来者的白人垃圾约翰·克雷森。

00:35:29.890 --> 00:36:01.920
<v Beau O'reilly>I hit him again anyway. And Jon Crescent was reluctant to hit me back. He kept pushing me away with this long basketball arms and not throwing any punches of his own. We were interrupted, finally, by Sister Edwardine. Her sharp caws of Stop it, stop it now!" broke the boys apart. I turned my head, so as to see her coming. She was short, full beaked in the nose, and bird-like. And I pictured her cawing above our heads, in my own odd head.
<v Beau O'reilly>我还是又打了他一下。约翰·克雷森不愿回击。他一直用他那双篮球长臂把我推开，没有挥拳。我们最终被爱德华丁修女打断了。她尖厉的“停下，立刻停下！”让男孩们散开了。我转过头，看着她走过来。她个子很矮，鼻子像鹰嘴，活像只鸟。我在自己的怪头里想象着她在我们头顶尖叫。

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:43.220
<v Beau O'reilly>And then John Crescent, who'd avoided hitting me back as long as everyone was watching, hit me as hard as he could. And I took the punch full in the face. It landed solidly on my left cheekbone. And I saw the Aurora Borealis, my head ringing like a fire drill. And as I landed on the pavement, I thought in my odd-headed way, of all the primitive, prehistoric men who went gaga in wonder at the first sight of the Northern Lights in a full sky. And then I hit the pavement, and I passed out.
<v Beau O'reilly>然后约翰·克雷森，在众目睽睽之下一直避免回击我，却用尽全力给了我一拳。我脸上结结实实地挨了这一拳。它落在我的左颧骨上。我看到了极光，头里响起了像火警一样的声音。当我倒在水泥地上时，我用那怪头怪脑的方式想到了所有那些在天空第一次看到北极光时惊叹不已的原始人类。然后我重重地撞在水泥地上，昏了过去。

00:36:43.220 --> 00:37:26.750
<v Beau O'reilly>The weather finally went hard cold the night before Christmas Eve. The lake froze solid. And I woke up to the early quiet, intending to go out and pick up the family Christmas tree. Our family having long ago figured out that a Christmas tree bought on the last day meant a substantial savings. Why I went alone that year, I don't remember. Usually, I would have gone with my multitude of brothers and sisters. But I did go alone, crossing the frozen lake. It was very cold. The wind like a wraith sucked all the heat from my body, before I reached the lot where the almost perfect seven-foot, short-needled pine trees still could be found.
<v Beau O'reilly>圣诞前夜，天气终于变得严寒。湖面彻底封冻。我在清晨的寂静中醒来，打算出去买家里的圣诞树。我们家很久以前就发现，在最后一天买圣诞树意味着能省下一大笔钱。为什么那年我独自一人，我记不清了。通常我会和我的兄弟姐妹们一起去。但我确实是一个人，穿过了冰封的湖面。天气非常冷。风像幽灵一样抽走了我身体里的热量，直到我到达那个仍能找到几乎完美的七英尺短针松树的摊位。

00:37:26.750 --> 00:38:00.230
<v Beau O'reilly>It was early still. And I had to knock and wake the trailer on the lot in order to buy the tree. The Christmas tree man may have been 40, but he looked 60, with three weeks of blue-black whiskers and a morning can of Budweiser. His eyes never looked at me as we negotiated the cost of the tree. But once we agreed, he yelled harshly to his still sleeping son to come out and trim the base of the tree to my specifications.
<v Beau O'reilly>时间还很早。我必须敲门叫醒摊位上的拖车房里的人才能买树。卖圣诞树的人可能才40岁，但他看起来像60岁，留着三周没修剪的蓝黑色胡茬，早晨就喝着一罐百威啤酒。当我们商定树的价格时，他的眼睛从来没正眼瞧过我。但我们达成协议后，他严厉地冲着还在睡觉的儿子大喊，让他出来按照我的规格修剪树底。

00:38:00.230 --> 00:38:32.130
<v Beau O'reilly>I was odd-heading and daydreaming about the amount of perfection in a seven-foot, short-needled pine tree, when the son came out, flinching as he walked past the Christmas tree guy. My thumb jumped and ached. He was John Crescent. And John Crescent was the son of the Christmas tree guy. My tree of choice was frozen to the ground. For a couple of minutes, the two of us had to struggle to work it free. And once we had, we stood for a moment in the morning cold.
<v Beau O'reilly>我当时正“怪头怪脑”地对七英尺短针松树的完美程度做着白日梦，这时他儿子出来了，走过卖树人身边时畏缩了一下。我的拇指一阵抽动和疼痛。他就是约翰·克雷森。约翰·克雷森是卖树人的儿子。我选中的那棵树冻在了地上。有几分钟，我们俩不得不费力地把它弄出来。一旦弄好，我们在早晨的寒冷中站了一会儿。

00:38:32.130 --> 00:39:04.760
<v Beau O'reilly>And I looked at John Crescent, full in the face. His left eye was swollen shut. His lip puffy and still traced with blood. And I had never hit him in the face. He had successfully kept me at a distance. And then I saw him in my own odd head. John Crescent, the Christmas tree guy's son, having to tell the Christmas tree guy about Sister Edwardine throwing him out of school after he'd knocked me out on the playground. And the Christmas tree guy, full of Budweiser, knocking John Crescent around some.
<v Beau O'reilly>我看着约翰·克雷森的脸。他的左眼肿得闭上了。嘴唇红肿，还带着血迹。我从来没打过他的脸。他成功地让我保持了距离。然后我在自己的怪头里看到了他：约翰·克雷森，卖树人的儿子，不得不告诉他父亲，在他把我在操场上打昏后，爱德华丁修女是怎么把他开除出校的。而卖树人，喝得醉醺醺的，把约翰·克雷森揍了一顿。

00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:37.040
<v Beau O'reilly>If John Crescent knew who I was, he had given no sign, staring out of his one good eye like I was a car wreck and him the insurance adjuster. But as my head filled with pity for him and remorse over our recent playground battle, he reached out forcefully and grabbed my still-bandaged thumb in his fist. Merry Christmas, he cawed, and he squeezed it. Merry Christmas. I shivered all the way home.
<v Beau O'reilly>如果约翰·克雷森知道我是谁，他一点没表现出来，他用那只唯一睁得开的眼睛盯着我，就像我是场车祸，而他是保险理赔员。但当我心中充满对他的同情以及对我们最近那场操场之战的懊悔时，他用力伸出手，紧紧抓住了我那缠着绷带的拇指。圣诞快乐，他嘶哑地说，并捏了捏。圣诞快乐。我一路发抖回到了家。

00:39:37.040 --> 00:42:10.730
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[SINGING WITH BAND]
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[乐队伴奏演唱]

00:42:10.730 --> 00:42:30.310
<v Ira Glass>Music from Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church on Chicago's South Side, 82nd and Cornell. It's Your Radio Playhouse . I'm Ira Glass here in WBEZ. More of this very music as our program continues. And a visit to the church, and what they're celebrating this Christmas, later in our show.
<v Ira Glass>这是来自芝加哥南区，第82街和康奈尔大道的信仰会幕浸信会教堂的音乐。这里是《您的广播剧场》。我是主持人艾拉·格拉斯，在WBEZ电台。随着节目的继续，会有更多这样的音乐。节目稍后，我们将拜访那座教堂，看看他们这个圣诞节在庆祝什么。

00:42:30.310 --> 00:42:55.540
<v Ira Glass>Just a couple weeks ago, one of the producers, Delores Wilber, and I went down to the Daniel J. Nellum Youth Services Incorporated. It's a place where teenage boys stay, mostly wards of the state, in fact, entirely wards of the state. And these are kids who can't be placed anywhere else, having trouble getting placed. They all live there. And there are writing classes there. And various arts classes.
<v Ira Glass>就在几周前，制作人多洛雷斯·威尔伯和我去了丹尼尔·J·内勒姆青年服务公司。那是一个少年管教所，住着主要是受国家监护的青少年，实际上，全是受国家监护的。这些孩子没法安置在别处，安置起来有困难。他们都住在那儿。那里有写作课，还有各种艺术课。

00:42:55.540 --> 00:43:12.160
<v Ira Glass>The writing classes, right now, are led by a local poet named Quraysh Ali. And Delores Wilber and I went to see what the boys' writing was like, maybe have some of them read on the show. And, in future weeks, we hope to have some of them read on the show.
<v Ira Glass>目前的写作课由一位名叫古莱什·阿里的当地诗人负责。多洛雷斯·威尔伯和我去看了看男孩们的写作水平如何，也许能让其中一些人在节目中朗读。在未来的几周里，我们希望邀请他们中的一些人来节目中朗读。

00:43:12.160 --> 00:43:37.910
<v Ira Glass>And one of these we were thinking about when we went down there is maybe they could write about Christmas. Maybe they'd have some experience to write about Christmas. And so when we were there and talking to them and seeing the writing, at one point we did a little writing exercise. And asked, in this writing exercise, for each of the boys to write something about Christmas. And this turned out to be a dreadful mistake. Because most of them had nothing pleasant to say about Christmas.
<v Ira Glass>我们去的时候想到的其中一点是，也许他们可以写写圣诞节。也许他们有什么关于圣诞节的经历可以写。所以当我们去那里和他们交谈并看到他们的作品时，我们做了一个小小的写作练习。在这次练习中，我们要求每个男孩写点关于圣诞节的东西。结果发现这是一个糟糕的错误。因为他们大多数人对圣诞节没什么愉快的回忆。

00:43:37.910 --> 00:44:01.560
<v Ira Glass>We went through the usual things that one might ask, just to give people ideas for what you're going to write about, presents you'd gotten, things you'd want to give to somebody else, people you'd want to give something to, things you wanted to tell someone. And had them either write it as a little story or a letter to someone. This on was typical. I'm just going to read this very short essay. This person's said.
<v Ira Glass>我们过了一遍通常会问的问题，只是为了给人们提供写作灵感：你收到的礼物、你想给别人的东西、你想给某人东西的人、你想告诉某人的话。并让他们要么写成小故事，要么写给某人一封信。这一篇很典型。我只读一下这篇很短的文章。这个人说。

00:44:01.560 --> 00:44:15.350
<v Ira Glass>The thing I most wanted for Christmas was nothing, because I have nothing. That's why I'm here. The person I like to give something to is no one. I've always wanted to tell you that. I didn't want nothing. And that's OK.
<v Ira Glass>我圣诞节最想要的是什么都没有，因为我本来就一无所有。这就是我为什么在这里。我唯一想给东西的人是谁也没有。我一直想告诉你。我什么都不想要。这没关系。

00:44:15.350 --> 00:44:52.510
<v Ira Glass>Well Reginald Gibbons is writing about a lot of things right now. But one of them, at least, concerns boys in pretty bad situations. He's the author of Sweetbitter , his most recent novel and other works of poetry. He's the editor of Triquarterly Magazine . And for his most recent book, he is doing some research. He is hanging around a lot at the Juvenile Court Building here in Chicago. And so when we asked him if he wanted to write about Christmas, he decided to set his story there.
<v Ira Glass>雷金纳德·吉本斯现在正在写很多东西。但至少其中之一涉及处于非常糟糕处境的男孩。他是他最近的小说《甜蜜苦涩》及其他诗歌作品的作者。他是《季刊杂志》的编辑。为了他最近的一本书，他正在做一些研究。他经常在芝加哥的少年法庭大楼里晃荡。所以当我们问他是否想写写圣诞节时，他决定把故事设定在那里。

00:44:52.510 --> 00:45:24.630
<v Reginald Gibbons>A typical hearing or trial is like a brief, highly organized dance. The public defenders work from a table to one side of the small courtroom, the state's attorneys from another at the other side. The clerk calls the next case. Someone steps out of the closed doors to the waiting area and calls out the name of the minor respondent. The child is brought in. A long moment later, someone's approaching, judge, says an attorney, to placate the impatient man behind the bench. In come three women.
<v Reginald Gibbons>典型的听证会或审判就像一场简短、高度组织化的舞蹈。公设辩护人从狭小法庭一侧的桌子工作，州检察官在另一侧。书记员呼叫下一个案件。有人从关闭的门走到等候区，喊出未成年被告的名字。孩子被带进来。片刻之后，有人走近法官，说，律师，去安抚长椅后那个不耐烦的人。三名妇女走了进来。

00:45:24.630 --> 00:45:45.110
<v Reginald Gibbons>The judge says, his mother here? Yes, judge, the public defender says, indicating one of the women. And who is with mother, the judge asks. Grandmother and aunt, says the attorney. The judge says, aunt, please sit at  the back of the courtroom. And the woman goes there without saying a word. The other two wait, also silent.
<v Reginald Gibbons>法官说，他母亲在这里吗？是的，法官，公设辩护人说，指了指其中一位妇女。和母亲一起来的是谁？法官问。祖母和阿姨，律师说。法官说，阿姨，请坐在法庭后面。那个女人一言不发地走到后面。另外两个也静静地等着。

00:45:45.110 --> 00:46:10.810
<v Reginald Gibbons>Like the women who are here to stand with him, the child is black this time. Everyone else in this courtroom is white. Handcuffs have been removed from his wrists at the side door to the courtroom. And, as instructed by the uniformed officer who accompanies him, he stands at the center of the bench before the judge, his hands clasped behind his back and holding, in this case, a jacket, a tube of toothpaste, and a toothbrush.
<v Reginald Gibbons>和站在他身边的妇女一样，这个孩子这次是个黑人。法庭里的其他人都是白人。他在法庭侧门被解开了手铐。按照陪同他的穿制服警官的指示，他站在法官面前长椅的中央，双手扣在背后，手里拿着夹克、一支牙膏和一把牙刷。

00:46:10.810 --> 00:46:34.370
<v Reginald Gibbons>This legal episode takes only three minutes. The state's attorney speaks. The judge speaks. The public defender speaks. The person of whom they are speaking is not asked to speak and does not speak. Although they have had time in their service to learn somewhat the language that he speaks, they do not speak a language that's intelligible to him.
<v Reginald Gibbons>这个法律插曲只用了三分钟。州检察官发言。法官发言。公设辩护人发言。他们谈论的对象没有被要求发言，也没有发言。尽管他们有时间在职期间学了一些他所说的语言，但他们说的语言对他来说并不通顺。

00:46:34.370 --> 00:47:05.320
<v Reginald Gibbons>The public defender meets his client in a small, bare, interview room. The client is 14 years old, and has been held for two weeks, so far, on a very serious charge of violent crime. His hearing, which will take place in two more weeks, will determine whether he will be tried as a juvenile, in this building, and have some chance to straighten out and go free again eventually, or whether he'll be sent to the court at 26th street, to be tried as an adult.
<v Reginald Gibbons>公设辩护人在一间狭小、空荡的审讯室见了当事人。当事人14岁，因严重的暴力犯罪指控已被关押两周。他两周后进行的听证会将决定他是作为未成年人在本楼受审，还有机会改过自新并最终获释，还是会被送往26街的法庭，作为成年人受审。

00:47:05.320 --> 00:47:27.000
<v Reginald Gibbons>The boy is short, thin, he does not look directly at the public defender, his attorney, who is explaining to him what happened in the courtroom in those three minutes of the judge's time. It was decided that something will be decided. But this explanation too, is in the language of the courts. It does not make sense to the child.
<v Reginald Gibbons>男孩个子不高，很瘦，他没有直视他的律师，律师正在向他解释那三分钟法官时间里发生了什么。决定是会做出某种决定。但这解释也使用的是法院的语言。这对孩子来说毫无意义。

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:44.180
<v Reginald Gibbons>So in response to the explanation, which is that he is going to be given psychological tests, and he needs to be completely honest with the doctor, and then there will be another session before the judge to determine where he will be tried, he says, how long am  I going to be in here?
<v Reginald Gibbons>所以对于他将被接受心理测试，他需要对医生完全诚实，然后还会再有一次法官前的庭审来决定他在哪里受审的解释，他回答说，我还要在这里待多久？

00:47:44.180 --> 00:48:20.390
<v Reginald Gibbons>For his crime, if he is convicted as an adult, he will probably remain in prison for the rest of his life. His attorney says, that depends, but it's going to be a while. We have to convince the judge not to send you to 26th street, right? Right. You know how we do that? Oh, man. You know how we do that? You have to do two things. You have to not get written up for anything while you're here. And you have to do well in school upstairs. Are you doing well in school here?
<v Reginald Gibbons>对于他的罪行，如果作为成年人被定罪，他可能会在监狱里度过余生。他的律师说，那得看情况，但还需要一段时间。我们必须说服法官不要把你送到26街，对吧？对。你知道我们怎么做吗？哦，伙计。你知道我们怎么做吗？你必须做两件事。你在这里的时候不能因为任何事情被记过。你还得在楼上的学校表现良好。你在这里上学表现好吗？

00:48:20.390 --> 00:48:34.920
<v Reginald Gibbons>The child is not doing well. And he has already been written up once. People get in his face, he says. He says, he has a temper. All he really wants to know is how long will he be in here.
<v Reginald Gibbons>孩子表现并不好。而且他已经被记过一次了。人们找我茬，他说。他说，他脾气不好。他真正想知道的是还要在这里待多久。

00:48:34.920 --> 00:48:56.280
<v Reginald Gibbons>It could be a long time, his attorney says. How long? Am I going to get out for Christmas? No. No? He doesn't believe this man is an attorney. He says, he wants a good lawyer. He wants to pay $500 and have a good lawyer.
<v Reginald Gibbons>可能会是很长一段时间，他的律师说。多久？我圣诞节前能出去吗？不能。不能？他不相信这个人是律师。他说，他想要个好律师。他想付500美元请个好律师。

00:48:56.280 --> 00:49:31.270
<v Reginald Gibbons>His sleeves are too long. His pants are too big. He shifts in his chair again and again. The small, windowless, brick walled room doesn't fit him either. Is it more important to you, the attorney says, to react when someone is on you, so they know you won't tolerate that, or to not spend the rest of your life in jail? If the judge hears you've got written up, he's going to think that you're not taking this seriously. And if that's what he thinks, then he'll decide you need to go to 26th street, right?
<v Reginald Gibbons>他的袖子太长了。裤子太大了。他在椅子上动来动去。那个没有窗户、砖墙砌成的小房间也不适合他。律师说，是反应当有人找你茬时让他们知道你不会忍气吞声更重要，还是余生不去监狱坐牢更重要？如果法官听说你被记过了，他会认为你没把这事当回事。如果他那么想，他就会决定把你送到26街，对吧？

00:49:31.270 --> 00:50:04.190
<v Reginald Gibbons>The child looks at every corner of the room and not at his attorney. He fidgets violently in his chair and flings his arms to one side, then the other. His attorney says, someone gets in your face, you have to walk away from it, right? And in school, you need A's and B's, OK? So we can ask one of the teachers to come down to the courtroom and tell the judge that you shouldn't be tried at 26th street as an adult, right?
<v Reginald Gibbons>孩子看着房间的每一个角落，就是不看他的律师。他在椅子上烦躁不安，胳膊甩向一边，又甩向另一边。他的律师说，有人找你茬，你必须走开，对吧？在学校，你需要拿到A和B，好吗？这样我们才能请一位老师到法庭告诉法官你不应该在26街作为成年人受审，对吧？

00:50:04.190 --> 00:50:24.640
<v Reginald Gibbons>The child is taken back into the part of the building that is behind locks. After he is gone, his attorney says of him, he isn't going to make it. What he has to do is more than any adult could be reasonably expected to be able to do. And he's only 14.
<v Reginald Gibbons>孩子被带回了大楼后面有锁的部分。他走后，律师评价他说，他没戏了。他必须做的事情，超过了任何一个成年人能合理做到的。而他才14岁。

00:50:24.640 --> 00:51:00.950
<v Reginald Gibbons>The day proceeds, more trials, more hearings, the courthouse empties. The worlds, here and everywhere around here, go dark early at this time of year. After rush hour, whether at the known street corners where traffic goes steadily by slowly, or at the quiet corners, where nothing should be happening, the snow falling since late afternoon makes everything quiet. And the street is bright in that strange, nighttime, snow lit way, from the light of a few street lamps, home lights, headlights reflected up off the soft whiteness.
<v Reginald Gibbons>日子继续过着，更多的审判，更多的听证会，法院空了。在这每年的这个时候，这里以及周围的世界天黑得很早。晚高峰过后，无论是在交通缓慢平稳行驶的知名街角，还是不该发生什么的安静街角，从深夜开始飘落的雪花让一切都变得安静。从几盏街灯、住宅灯光、以及从柔软白雪上反射上来的车灯光中，街道在那种奇异的、深夜的雪色照耀下显得很明亮。

00:51:00.950 --> 00:51:21.330
<v Reginald Gibbons>But inside the juvenile detention center, it's still noisy. People are getting in other people's faces, taunting the weak, threatening, messing with their heads. Outside the dirty unbreakable windows, the snow is falling in the night, falling freely, softly, steadily, slowly.
<v Reginald Gibbons>但在少年拘留中心内部，依然很吵。人们正在找别人的茬，嘲弄弱者，恐吓，折磨着他们的精神。在脏兮兮、无法拆卸的窗外，雪在夜里落下，自由地落下，静静地、稳定地、缓慢地。

00:51:21.330 --> 00:52:34.740
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[SINGING WITH BAND]
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[乐队伴唱]

00:52:34.740 --> 00:52:56.830
<v Ira Glass>So we wanted a Christmas story for this week that really was more religiously based. And we wanted some Christmas music. And to get both, we decided to visit a church that had something very specific to celebrate this year, and a great choir with which to celebrate it. One of our production staff, Peter Clowney, went to Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church.
<v Ira Glass>本周我们想要一个更具宗教色彩的圣诞故事，同时也想要一些圣诞音乐。为了两者兼得，我们决定去访问一家今年有特殊庆祝活动，并且拥有出色唱诗班的教堂。我们的制作人员之一，彼得·克劳尼（Peter Clowney），去了信仰帐幕浸信会教堂（Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church）。

00:52:56.830 --> 00:53:13.250
<v Peter Clowney>The church meets in a smallish building on South Cornell, near 82nd and Stony Brook. It's in a middle class neighborhood, but there's some tougher areas nearby. By about 11 o'clock on Sunday morning maybe 50 people are occupying the warm second-floor sanctuary.
<v Peter Clowney>这座教堂位于南康奈尔大道（South Cornell）的一栋小建筑里，靠近第82街和斯托尼布鲁克（Stony Brook）。它处于一个中产阶级社区，但附近也有一些治安较差的地区。周日上午11点左右，大约50个人聚集在二楼温暖的礼拜堂里。

00:53:13.250 --> 00:53:29.910
<v Peter Clowney>They sit in groups, talking about their lives with Christ this week, about other things. And some people are praying. By 11:30, about 100 people are gathered this way. And when the praise and worship starts, music dissolves these little groups into one congregation.
<v Peter Clowney>他们三五成群地坐着，谈论着本周与基督同行的生活以及其他琐事。有些人正在祈祷。到了11点半，大约有100人聚集在这里。当赞美和敬拜开始时，音乐将这些小团体融合成了一个统一的会众。

00:53:29.910 --> 00:53:45.720
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[SINGING]
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[歌唱声]

00:53:45.720 --> 00:53:52.020
<v Peter Clowney>Reverend Donald Sharp begins the service at noon. He tells people to greet each other in the pews, to meet their neighbors.
<v Peter Clowney>唐纳德·夏普（Donald Sharp）牧师于正午开始礼拜。他让人们在座位上互相问候，认识身边的邻居。

00:53:52.020 --> 00:54:05.030
<v Rev. Donald Sharp>As a neighbor. If I want to be forgiven by God, I got to forgive you.
<v Rev. Donald Sharp>作为邻居。如果我想得到上帝的宽恕，我就必须宽恕你。

00:54:05.030 --> 00:54:37.170
<v Peter Clowney>I'm struck by how people greet each other here. My father was a minister in Georgia. Our church was half black and half white, but we were all Presbyterian. And, in service, when we greeted each other, we use to do it nervously. We'd brush fingertips and mumble at each other. A lot of us avoided eye contact. Here at Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church, people clasp each other's hands. They look straight in each other's eyes. They say, neighbor. This is a church where no one sits alone.
<v Peter Clowney>这里人们互相问候的方式让我深受触动。我父亲曾是佐治亚州的一位牧师。我们的教堂有一半是黑人，一半是白人，但我们都是长老会教徒。在礼拜中，我们互相问候时总是很拘谨。我们会碰碰指尖，含糊地嘀咕几句。我们许多人避免目光接触。而在信仰帐幕浸信会教堂，人们紧紧握住对方的手。他们直视对方的眼睛。他们说，邻居。这是一个没有人会独自坐着的教堂。

00:54:37.170 --> 00:55:03.490
<v Peter Clowney>Calvin Bridges is the choir director. He is an athletic looking man in his mid-30s. He's got this sculpted beard. On Wednesdays, at choir rehearsal, he is all motion. He runs from organ over to the choir. He sings parts for them. He's sweating in this orange t-shirt. But during Sunday service, in his grey suit, he stands behind the organ and directs the choir from there, with his singing and by punching up the beat on the organ.
<v Peter Clowney>卡尔文·布里奇斯（Calvin Bridges）是唱诗班指挥。他是一位30多岁、身材健硕的男士，留着修剪整齐的胡子。周三彩排时，他活力四射。他从管风琴跑到唱诗班那边，为他们唱分部。他穿着橙色T恤，汗流浃背。但在周日的礼拜中，他穿着灰色西装，站在管风琴后指挥，用歌声和敲击管风琴的节奏来引导唱诗班。

00:55:03.490 --> 00:55:17.640
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[SINGING]
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[歌唱声]

00:55:17.640 --> 00:56:02.630
<v Peter Clowney>And the choir this morning is about 20 people, maybe five teenage boys who don't seem to be into it, a few young women who are, a couple men over 30. but mostly it's women in their 30s and 40s. They seem really to get along. The closest I saw them come to disagreeing was after rehearsal Wednesday night. Traci Kiraten, one of choir members, told the group about the dance school that she's starting with her mom and sister. They're going to be teaching tap and jazz, maybe some aerobics, and there is also going to be martial arts. Choir director Bridges is an ordained minister, he's a very devout man. He puller her aside and told her, martial arts could be an instrument of Satan. They came to an understanding.
<v Peter Clowney>今天早上的唱诗班大约有20人，可能有五个看起来不太上心的十几岁男孩，几位投入的年轻女性，还有几个30多岁的男人，但大多是30到40岁的女性。她们相处得很好。我看到她们最接近意见不合的一次是周三晚上彩排后。唱诗班成员之一特蕾西·基拉滕（Traci Kiraten）告诉大家，她要和妈妈、妹妹开办一家舞蹈学校。她们打算教踢踏舞和爵士舞，可能还有健美操，此外还有武术。唱诗班指挥布里奇斯是一位授职牧师，他非常虔诚。他把她拉到一旁说，武术可能是撒旦的工具。最终他们达成了一致。

00:56:02.630 --> 00:56:25.100
<v Traci Kiraten>Martial arts does have a background with, I think, it's the Far East religions like Buddhism. I'm glad that he asked that question. But my response to him was that the teacher, I'm not the teacher, but the teacher that does teach the martial art classes, he has a Christian background. And he is a ordained minister.
<v Traci Kiraten>武术确实有背景，我想是来自佛教等远东宗教。我很高兴他问了那个问题。但我的回答是，老师——我不是老师，但教武术课的老师——他有基督教背景。而且他是一位受封的牧师。

00:56:25.100 --> 00:56:50.940
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[SINGING]
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[歌唱声]

00:56:50.940 --> 00:57:07.950
<v Peter Clowney>At this church they don't make a big deal out of Christmas. There are no real decorations around. Maybe two Christmas songs in their entire morning service. And when I asked people about it, they told me, they celebrate Christ year round. So why should Christmas be any different.
<v Peter Clowney>在这个教堂，他们并不太看重圣诞节。周围没有真正的装饰。整个晨间礼拜可能只有两首圣诞歌曲。当我问起时，他们告诉我，他们一年四季都在庆祝基督，所以圣诞节有什么不同呢。

00:57:07.950 --> 00:57:22.540
<v Angela Cook>I give God praise whether it be Christmas, Easter. I just don't take one day out of the year to celebrate. I celebrate all the time. Hallelujah, because God is good. Yes he is. He's good. I'm getting excited.
<v Angela Cook>无论是圣诞节还是复活节，我都赞美上帝。我不会只在一年中的某一天庆祝。我时刻都在庆祝。哈利路亚，因为上帝是美好的。没错，他是。他是美好的。我很兴奋。

00:57:22.540 --> 00:57:27.920
<v Peter Clowney>That's Angela Cook, a member of the choir for 25 years. She's one of the soloists.
<v Peter Clowney>那是安吉拉·库克（Angela Cook），她加入唱诗班已经25年了。她是独唱歌手之一。

00:57:27.920 --> 00:57:56.020
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[ANGELA COOK SINGING WITH BAND AND CHOIR]
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[安吉拉·库克与乐队及唱诗班合唱]

00:57:56.020 --> 00:58:04.850
<v Peter Clowney>4:30, we're back at the church for the mortgage burn. First, everyone takes battery powered candles and marches around the building.
<v Peter Clowney>4点半，我们回到教堂参加“抵押贷款偿清仪式”。首先，每个人拿着电池驱动的蜡烛绕着建筑游行。

00:58:04.850 --> 00:58:19.530
<v Rev. Donald Sharp>[SINGING ] We've come this far by faith. Leaning on the Lord. Trusting in his holy word.
<v Rev. Donald Sharp>[歌唱声] 我们凭信心走到今天。依靠主。信靠他神圣的道。

00:58:19.530 --> 00:58:50.520
<v Peter Clowney>Reverend Sharp founded this church 31 years ago, with 12 people. Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church now has 500 members, too many for this building. And last year, Reverend Sharp made a decision. He told his congregation, look, we're going to pay $90,000 left on our mortgage. Not by the year 2004, like we're supposed to, but now. Because once we've done that, we can start building. We can build a place that can hold all of us.
<v Peter Clowney>夏普牧师在31年前创立了这座教堂，当时只有12个人。信仰帐幕浸信会教堂现在有500名成员，这栋建筑已经容纳不下了。去年，夏普牧师做了一个决定。他告诉会众，看，我们要付清剩余的9万美元贷款。不是像计划那样在2004年付清，而是现在。因为一旦做到这一点，我们就可以开始扩建。我们可以建造一个能容纳我们所有人的地方。

00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:53.270
<v Peter Clowney>A year later, even Reverend Sharp is surprised that they did it.
<v Peter Clowney>一年后，连夏普牧师都对他们做到了这一点感到惊讶。

00:58:53.270 --> 00:59:18.890
<v Rev. Donald Sharp>When we began last year to make a determination that we were going to engage in this campaign, I had to be very honest with you, I had some serious doubts. Not about God, but about you. Amen. I'm might as well, might as well be honest with you.
<v Rev. Donald Sharp>去年当我们开始决定要参与这个活动时，我必须诚实地说，我有一些严重的怀疑。不是怀疑上帝，而是怀疑你们。阿门。我最好还是对你们诚实一点。

00:59:18.890 --> 00:59:35.240
<v Peter Clowney>As of last Sunday, Faith Tabernacle has raised $101,000. That's an incredible amount given how many people they are, and what people there make. It comes out to about $2,000 per person, on top of tithing, on top of the mission funds that they give.
<v Peter Clowney>截至上周日，信仰帐幕教堂已经筹集了101,000美元。考虑到他们的成员人数以及大家的收入，这是一笔惊人的数字。平均每人捐了约2000美元，这还不包括什一奉献和宣教基金。

00:59:35.240 --> 01:00:25.740
<v Rev. Donald Sharp>And in a year's time, we didn't sell not one chicken, didn't sell not one banquet ticket, didn't sell anything. I could stand here and tell you the story as how we used to believe in selling dinners, banquets. And how folks would be mad at each other around here. Because you didn't buy no ticket from me, I ain't going to buy no ticket from you. All right. And how we used to have banquets and used to have ticket selling. And somehow the tickets and the money didn't come out right.
<v Rev. Donald Sharp>在这一年里，我们没有卖过一只鸡，没有卖过一张宴会票，什么都没卖。我可以在这里告诉你们我们过去是如何相信靠卖晚餐、办宴会筹款的。以及人们在那期间是如何因为“你没买我的票，我就不买你的票”而彼此生气。没错。我们过去常办宴会，常卖票。但不知何故，票款和账目总是对不上。

01:00:25.740 --> 01:01:13.260
<v Peter Clowney>Anyone who's been part of a church, knows that no big moment in a church's life is going to pass without dozens of people speaking, and without a service that goes on perhaps a bit too long. By about 7:30 people are fidgeting. Teenagers in the back slump in the pews. But then, finally, the moment arrives. 20 or so leaders of the church gather at the pulpit, their backs to the congregation. They lay the mortgage in an alter that appears to be covered with tinfoil. It burns surprisingly quickly with a tiny wisp of smoke, while some people sing, burn, out loud.
<v Peter Clowney>任何在教堂待过的人都知道，教堂生活中的重大时刻总少不了几十个人发言，仪式也总是会拖得有点长。到了7点半左右，人们开始坐立不安。后排的青少年在长椅上瘫倒。但随后，时刻终于到来。约20名教会领袖聚集在讲坛前，背对着会众。他们将抵押贷款凭证放在一个看起来盖着锡纸的祭坛上。它烧得异常迅速，只留下一丝青烟，同时一些人高声唱着“燃烧”。

01:01:13.260 --> 01:01:34.440
<v Peter Clowney>Christmas, in a certain sense, is about tomorrow. It's about hope. The child was is just born in the darkness of winter. He is bringing light with him. It's the perfect moment for a group of people who've stood with each other for 30 years to look to the future, to begin to build.
<v Peter Clowney>从某种意义上说，圣诞节是关于未来的。它是关于希望的。这个孩子出生在冬日的黑暗中。他带来了光明。对于一群相互扶持了30年的人来说，这是一个看向未来、开始建设的完美时刻。

01:01:34.440 --> 01:02:08.180
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[SINGING REPETION OF THANK YOU]
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[重复唱着“谢谢你”]

01:02:08.180 --> 01:02:09.600
<v Ira Glass>Well, thank you this evening.
<v Ira Glass>那么，今晚谢谢大家。

01:02:09.600 --> 01:02:18.630
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>[资助名单]

01:02:18.630 --> 01:02:29.630
<v Ira Glass>Today's show was produced by Nancy Updike and myself, with Alix Spiegel, Peter Clowney, and Delores Wilber. Contributing editors Jack Hitt, Margie Rochlin and Paul Tough. We broadcast from WBEZ Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目由南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）和我本人制作，艾丽克斯·斯皮格尔（Alix Spiegel）、彼得·克劳尼（Peter Clowney）和多洛雷斯·威尔伯（Delores Wilber）参与制作。特约编辑：杰克·希特（Jack Hitt）、玛吉·罗克林（Margie Rochlin）和保罗·塔夫（Paul Tough）。我们从芝加哥WBEZ电台播出。

01:02:29.630 --> 01:02:37.290
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[鸣谢]

01:02:37.290 --> 01:02:48.060
<v Ira Glass>The choir director, Calvin Bridges does have a record coming out, a CD, of the Chicago Praise Ensemble, some people from the church and some other people, from their recent tours in Paris and Scandinavia. Look for that.
<v Ira Glass>唱诗班指挥卡尔文·布里奇斯确实要发行一张CD，收录了芝加哥赞美乐团（Chicago Praise Ensemble）的作品，成员包括来自教堂和近期巴黎及斯堪的纳维亚巡演的其他人员。敬请期待。

01:02:48.060 --> 01:03:00.090
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS CONT.] I'm Ira Glass. See you next week.
<v Ira Glass>[鸣谢续] 我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。下周见。

01:03:00.090 --> 01:03:05.090
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[SINGING]
<v Faith Tabernacle Baptist Church Choir>[歌唱声]
